Provenance
----------

dtls — clean-room implementation from RFC 9147 (Datagram Transport Layer
Security 1.3) and RFC 8446 (TLS 1.3 — shared key-schedule and handshake
message shapes, reused by DTLS 1.3 per RFC 9147 §5.8/§5.3/§5.4; no TLS 1.3
code copied, only the specification's design reused as RFC 9147 itself
mandates). Both are open, public IETF specifications — not copyrightable
works (merger doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no third-party-source
NOTICE entry is strictly required for the RFC citations alone.

This module additionally names the real design/code references consulted:
Zig 0.16 std's `std.crypto.tls` (`lib/std/crypto/tls.zig`, MIT, part of Zig
itself) — its `hello_retry_request_sequence` magic-value constant (RFC 8446
§4.1.3) is re-exported directly (a spec-mandated public value), and its
`hkdfExpandLabel` was read for reference. Note this module does NOT call
`std.crypto.tls.hkdfExpandLabel`: that function hardcodes the TLS `"tls13 "`
label prefix, whereas DTLS 1.3 (RFC 9147 §5.9) requires `"dtls13"`, so the
`HkdfLabel` construction is re-implemented here (`keyschedule.expandLabel`)
with a parameterized prefix. No source was copied from
`std.crypto.tls.Client`'s private handshake state machine.

The AEAD primitives (`std.crypto.aead.aes_gcm`, `chacha_poly`), the
single-block AES-ECB (`std.crypto.core.aes`), the ChaCha20 block keystream
(`std.crypto.stream.chacha`), HKDF/HMAC, and SHA-256/384 are used via their
public std APIs (MIT, part of Zig). Verification used RFC 8448 "Example
Handshake Traces for TLS 1.3" (IETF, public) as key-schedule KATs and the
`cryptography` package (OpenSSL) as an independent AEAD/mask oracle at test
time — neither is linked or vendored.

**Status: crypto core implemented + validated; handshake flight engine
deferred.** `keyschedule.zig`/`aead.zig` are real and KAT-validated;
`Connection.zig`'s application-data record path is real. The record header,
handshake framing/fragmentation, flight/ACK logic, and message-body framing
(`record.zig`, `handshake.zig`, `flight.zig`, `messages.zig`) are real and
tested. `Connection.startHandshake` (the handshake flight engine) is not yet
built.

Committed test data
-------------------

Everything under `src/testdata/` was produced by this repository's own tooling
on this repository's own machines; none of it is reproduced from a third party,
so none of it carries an upstream condition.

  * `src/testdata/certs/*.der`, `*.bin` — the fixture X.509 chain and its raw
    private scalars, generated with OpenSSL 3.5.5 (`openssl req -x509 …` /
    `openssl x509 -req … -CA …`). They are on disk rather than as hex literals
    in `src/certauth_kat_vectors.zig` because the live interop program, which
    is outside this module and cannot import across the package root, must hand
    wolfSSL the SAME bytes the Zig side trusts. Test material only; used
    nowhere outside this repository's tests.
  * `src/testdata/wolfssl_transcript.txt` — a capture of this module's own
    handshakes against a locally installed wolfSSL 5.9.1, recorded by
    `tools/interop.zig`. It contains bytes wolfSSL emitted on a loopback
    socket; it contains no wolfSSL source, and wolfSSL is neither linked nor
    vendored here.

`tools/wolfssl_peer.c` is original work written in this repository against
wolfSSL's public API; it is not part of the shipped module and is compiled
only by `zig build interop-dtls`.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.
